(function () {
  var m = {"SEC202": {"abstract": "In this 20-minute lightning talk, see how AWS Security Hub Extended correlates signals across endpoint, identity, email, data, browser, and AI domains into a single attack narrative. We show how cross-domain correlation surfaces attack vectors invisible to any single tool, and how customers deploy preconfigured detection patterns for immediate value.", "language": "en"},"SEC203": {"abstract": "Many organizations do not operate in a single cloud provider, creating the need for security solutions that cover multiple providers. In this lightning talk, see how AWS security services deliver unified multicloud security across AWS, Azure, and GCP environments. From posture management and vulnerability detection to risk correlation and asset visibility, learn how security teams can gain a consolidated view and respond to risks across their entire cloud estate.", "language": "en"},"SEC204": {"abstract": "Post-quantum migrations succeed when organizations can discover cryptographic assets, assign ownership, and continuously manage risk. Organisations are facing a rapid increase in machine identities, service identities, APIs, containers, AI workloads, and other cryptographic assets. At the same time, certificate lifetimes continue to shrink, making manual lifecycle management increasingly unsustainable. AWS and DXC Technology show how enterprise governance connects with crypto agility. See how DXC built a cryptographic asset intelligence and orchestration platform on AWS to discover and correlate assets across AWS and enterprise PKI, automate certificate lifecycles, and assess post-quantum readiness. A live proof of concept demonstrates inventory, prioritization, and continuous reassessment. Leave with an operating model aligned with NIST and CNSA 2.0 timelines.", "language": "en"},"SEC205": {"abstract": "You use certificates for identity, keys for token signing, and long-lived roots of trust. Now you need to migrate to post-quantum cryptography (PQC) for authentication. In this session, learn how AWS helps you start and stay PQ-ready while preserving backwards compatibility, performance, and AI-friendly security by default. We will demystify the present state and future trajectory of PQ authentication. We discuss the updated CNSA 2.0 dates when RSA and EC will no longer satisfy authentication requirements and the uncertainty around PQ public certificate formats and Merkle Trees in non-browser scenarios.", "language": "en"},"SEC206": {"abstract": "New regulations and geopolitical shifts are pushing organizations to rethink how they protect data and control access in the cloud, while still adopting AI. This session covers how AWS helps customers meet digital sovereignty requirements without giving up capability, through offerings that include the AWS European Sovereign Cloud and controls you can verify and enforce. You learn what AI sovereignty means in practice, get answers to the questions customers ask most, and hear how AWS and partners help organizations meet sovereignty requirements as they change.", "language": "en"},"SEC207": {"abstract": "How can we trust AWS with our payment keys? Join AWS payment security specialists and Capital One as they break down the controls and compliance behind cloud-based payment cryptography. Learn how AWS built trust through transparency, openly sharing its security architecture in public documentation and validating compliance through rigorous third-party assessments. As a result Capital One offloads infrastructure security to AWS and channels its energy into application-level protection and payment innovation that moves the business forward.", "language": "en"},"SEC208": {"abstract": "With so many security best practices available, knowing what to do first is a challenge. In this session, you learn how to use the AWS Security Maturity Model to systematically identify and prioritize security improvements for your AWS workloads through a phased approach. You discover how to extend your governance capabilities to manage security consistently across multi-cloud environments. Finally, you see how automatic evidence collection helps you continuously demonstrate compliance to auditors, turning security posture into provable assurance.", "language": "en"},"SEC210-S": {"abstract": "Agent permissions offer two bad options: approve every action manually, or skip permissions and trust the prompt. Auto-approve protects your laptop; it won't stop an agent from deleting a thousand CRM records. Roles and scopes suit static users, not agents whose next action depends on the last prompt. This session introduces Intent-Based Access Control (IBAC), where the agent's intent, not a pre-assigned role, is the unit of permission. WorkOS built Airlock on it: mission-bound tokens, every call evaluated against intent, decisions resolving to allow, deny, or escalate. Live demo across Gmail and Linear, including a blocked exfiltration and real-time human approval. This presentation is brought to you by WorkOS, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC211-S": {"abstract": "As AI accelerates development in the cloud, it’s also increasingly expanding the cloud attack surface. With autonomous agents accelerating both software deployment and vulnerability exploitation, traditional security workflows can no longer keep pace. Organizations need a new operating model for AI Threat Readiness inside high-velocity AWS environments that builds agentic trust without slowing innovation. This session explores how combining full AWS visibility with agentic workflows, including Wiz Red, Green, and Blue Agents, transforms reactive cloud security into proactive, machine-speed defense across Amazon Bedrock, containers, and serverless architectures. This presentation is brought to you by Wiz, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC212-S": {"abstract": "Tighten the detection rule and you miss the variant. Loosen them and you drown the on-call. Deviate too far either way and you risk missing attackers and getting compromised. FanDuel handled adjustments by hand for years, until attackers started moving faster than the tuning backlog. Join FanDuel and Datadog to see detection rebuilt around automation: SIEM logic as code, orchestrated enrichment and triage, vulnerability workflows in the same pipeline. Attendees will leave with practical patterns for scaling detection engineering, reducing analyst toil, and knowing where AI helps and where it creates new work. This presentation is brought to you by Datadog, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC213-S": {"abstract": "AI is multiplying the identities and agents that can change your AWS data. One over-scoped role or runaway agent can delete or overwrite the data your models depend on, in an S3 lake or Iceberg lakehouse. Replication copies a bad write, versioning is often off. How do you recover without a valid backup? Learn where recovery breaks across S3, Glue Data Catalog, S3 Tables, and databases and pipelines around them, then how Rubrik recovers petabytes in hours, restoring only what changed, critical data first, within your RTO. Resilience spans your estate. Hear how TGS built a ransomware-resilient platform with Rubrik. This presentation is brought to you by Rubrik, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC214-S": {"abstract": "Abstract: AI-driven attacks move at machine speed, and your AWS application is an interconnected system of code, infrastructure and data. Rebuilding by hand is a nightmare, if it’s even possible. Join us to see how an AI attack unfolds, and how Rubrik brings your business back by recovering the whole application in one automated motion. Rubrik discovers your application's dependencies, flags structural drift before it becomes an incident, and recovers it all together. Hear how Just Eat is moving from manual to validated, isolated recovery on AWS. Outpace AI-speed attacks with machine-speed recovery: less downtime, no manual rebuilds. This presentation is brought to you by Rubrik, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC215-S": {"abstract": "This session explores the evolution of cloud security and why traditional approaches fall short in the era of ephemeral compute and agentic AI. While legacy CloudSec relied on static scanning for visibility, today’s environments are dynamic, real-time, and increasingly autonomous, resulting in blind spots, excessive noise, and incomplete protection. We’ll examine how AI-driven and hybrid cloud architectures introduce new challenges across visibility, protection, and validation, from cross-provider gaps to real-time attack vectors and model vulnerabilities. To address this, Cloud and AI security must move beyond static analysis to a unified, runtime-driven approach. By continuously monitoring activity across the environment and embedding security throughout the SDLC, including production, organizations gain real-time context, better prioritization, and proactive threat mitigation. This presentation is brought to you by Upwind, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC216-S": {"abstract": "Defenders often drown in noise while attackers move at machine speed. To truly secure your environment, you must adopt an attacker’s perspective. Horizon3 CEO Snehal Antani shares insights from 250,000+ autonomous pentests across 6,000+ environments, demonstrating how AI hackers find exploitable paths that scanners miss, such as credential theft and domain compromise without CVEs. You’ll learn why vulnerable doesn’t mean exploitable, how to validate controls through a Hack-Fix-Verify loop, and where AI belongs in offense and defense: tool for scale and proof, not a black box. This presentation is brought to you by Horizon3.ai, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC217-S": {"abstract": "AI agents are evolving from assistants into autonomous systems that call APIs and take actions on behalf of users. Traditional perimeter controls aren't enough; threats now emerge from prompt injection, excessive permissions, and unmonitored tool use. In this session, we'll show how Check Point's AI security intelligence integrates with Amazon Bedrock AgentCore to enforce real-time guardrails at the agent runtime layer inspecting intent, validating actions, and preventing policy violations before they execute. Walk away with a practical framework for securing agentic AI on AWS with Check Point. This presentation is brought to you by Check Point, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC218-S": {"abstract": "Network and edge controls stop the traffic patterns they were built to recognize. AI agents introduce a different pattern: authenticated requests, at machine speed, against business logic rather than known vulnerabilities. Teams running AWS WAF often find these requests pass inspection because nothing about them is malformed. In this session, examine how agent-driven traffic reaches APIs, which risks fall outside signature and rate-based inspection, and what an additive API security layer contributes. Learn how teams instrument existing AWS WAF deployments for API-level visibility, apply real-time redaction to sensitive data in responses, and enforce without adding latency. This presentation is brought to you by Wallarm, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC219-S": {"abstract": "Agentic AI can transform how utilities serve customers, manage infrastructure and respond to operational events. Join IBM Consulting and industry leaders to see how organizations are putting agentic AI to work on AWS across complex operational workflows. Through real client stories, explore how modern platforms, trusted data and secure software foundations help teams improve service, strengthen resilience and move AI into production with confidence. This presentation is brought to you by IBM, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC220-S": {"abstract": "An AI agent can be easy to prototype but hard to take to production once it needs access to real systems. For developers, platform engineers, and security teams building with AWS AgentCore, this session shows how 1Password uses the same governed credential workflow from a local sandbox to the cloud without code changes. Attendees will see how this approach helps engineering teams move faster, limits the blast radius of a compromised agent, and gives security teams a clear audit trail. The session covers short-lived credential delivery through the AgentCore gateway and policies that apply consistently from development through production. This presentation is brought to you by 1Password, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC221-S": {"abstract": "AI is accelerating how fast code ships and how fast attackers find exploitable weaknesses, and annual pentests cannot keep pace. This session looks at what it takes to make security validation genuinely continuous rather than just more frequent, tracking what changed instead of retesting everything from scratch. Self-driving cars, robotic surgery, and other sensitive actions all became viable the same way: not by removing the human, but by defining exactly which actions the machine takes on its own and which ones need a hand on the controls. This talk will cover the delegate balance between autonomy, safety and control in Offensive Security. This presentation is brought to you by Terra, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC222-S": {"abstract": "In this session, we will demonstrate how AI is redefining cloud security by moving beyond static posture management to real-time, context-aware protection. The session will showcase how organizations can gain continuous visibility across their environments and respond to threats as they emerge. Attendees will see how AI-driven analysis of runtime behavior enables faster detection of anomalies, prioritization of real risks, and automated response across dynamic, multi-cloud infrastructures. The session will highlight how security teams can reduce noise, strengthen security processes, and proactively respond to threats before they escalate. This presentation is brought to you by Upwind, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC223-S": {"abstract": "A confirmed exploit still needs a code fix, and that fix often takes days or weeks. The exploit path stays open the whole time. This session shows how Terra Security turns a validated pentest finding into a scoped virtual patch inside the application's existing AWS WAF: matched to the specific attack, not a site-wide block, activated by a human on your team, and checked against both the exploit and normal traffic before you trust it. You will leave with a clear picture of how the integration works and a model for closing the gap between proof and fix without breaking the app you are protecting. This presentation is brought to you by Terra, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC224-S": {"abstract": "Most security tools score vulnerabilities in isolation, but attackers do not think in isolation. This session shows how Terra continuously chains signals into real attack paths across web applications, APIs, AI systems, and network infrastructure using agentic AI, the same way a human adversary would pivot from one weak point to the next. Every proposed chain is validated by an expert human pentester before it becomes a finding, turning a pile of low severity noise into a small number of confirmed, exploitable risks. Learn how continuous, cross surface attack chaining changes what a security team should actually prioritize first. This presentation is brought to you by Terra, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC225-S": {"abstract": "AI is rapidly moving from experimentation to production, creating new challenges for developers, architects, and security teams. In this technical session, explore the real-world architectures enterprises use to build and secure AI applications on AWS. Learn how organizations leverage Amazon Bedrock, EKS, ECS, Security Lake, CloudTrail, and cloud-native automation to protect AI workloads, monitor risk across multi-account environments, and streamline security operations. Through practical deployment patterns and lessons learned from large-scale AWS environments, discover how to build AI applications that are secure, scalable, and production-ready. The session draws on deployment patterns described for TrendAI Vision One on AWS, including AI workload protection, cloud telemetry integration, and AWS-native architecture approaches. This presentation is brought to you by TrendAI™, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC226-S": {"abstract": "Separate guardrails for humans, machine identities, and AI agents get messy, and rewriting every IAM policy takes forever. SCPs deployed at scope are easy to write. What follows is not. Nobody can say what a policy will break, so it never deploys. Someone gets blocked and the fix becomes a two hour hunt. The tenth control behaves nothing like the first. New accounts inherit none of it. This lightning talk answers all four using only AWS Organizations, SCPs, and RCPs. See how usage data makes a default deny safe, how access returns in seconds, and how least privilege holds. This presentation is brought to you by Sonrai Security, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC227-S": {"abstract": "Every day, employees install browser extensions, IDE plugins, and open-source packages with powerful access to corporate devices, code, credentials, and data. AI coding agents are accelerating this practice by recommending and installing software to complete tasks. Yet much of it enters the enterprise without comprehensive security review. We've normalized a practice that may someday seem as unthinkable as smoking on airplanes. In this session, we'll expose hidden risks in plugin and package ecosystems, show why traditional application controls miss this rapidly changing software layer, and explore what security teams must rethink as AI makes software adoption faster and increasingly autonomous. This presentation is brought to you by Glow, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC228-S": {"abstract": "LLMs didn't kill security testing, they exposed tools that guess at context they don't have. This talk explains what we learned by reinventing program analysis for new end users: builders and agents. We had to develop benchmarks and add determinism with program slicing to better control costs and deliver the optimal price per true vulnerability detected. This presentation is brought to you by Semgrep, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC229-S": {"abstract": "AI is reshaping cybersecurity, but security teams must act faster without adding complexity. Sempra Energy, AWS, and Accenture deployed Accenture Cyber.AI on AWS to create a unified view of cyber risk and identify the highest-priority actions across the enterprise. Learn how the team integrated existing security investments, addressed governance and architecture requirements, and operationalized AI-driven risk management. Speakers will share key design decisions, implementation lessons, and practical guidance for using AI to improve cyber resilience, strengthen security operations, and maintain control of data and infrastructure in cloud environments. This presentation is brought to you by Accenture, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC230-S": {"abstract": "Digital sovereignty is a growing priority for global enterprises operating across Europe as they navigate regulatory requirements, geopolitical uncertainty, and increasing demand for cloud and AI innovation. Organizations must balance data residency, compliance, resilience, and business agility without slowing transformation. Learn how leading enterprises are using AWS European Sovereign Cloud as part of a broader cloud strategy to meet sovereignty requirements while continuing to adopt modern cloud services and AI. Accenture will share practical approaches to assessing sovereignty needs, designing compliant architectures, and integrating sovereign cloud capabilities into enterprise environments. Attendees will leave with actionable guidance for balancing trust, resilience, compliance, and innovation at scale. This presentation is brought to you by Accenture, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC231-S": {"abstract": "With the rise of autonomous AI agents like Devin, Cursor, and Amazon Q, the security boundary has shifted from the network perimeter directly to the developer's workspace. These AI tools and automated integration pipelines establish dynamic, encrypted connections straight to GitHub, third-party repositories, and cloud workloads—creating blind spots that legacy firewalls cannot see. Join us as we map out this highly interconnected AI developer ecosystem and showcase why traditional security fails. We will share a unified, proxy-based Zero Trust architecture that inspects encrypted developer traffic, secures access to critical repositories, and isolates untrusted AI actions. You’ll leave with an actionable blueprint to protect your code, APIs, and AI integrations while maintaining peak developer velocity. This presentation is brought to you by Zscaler, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC232-S": {"abstract": "Building AI agents is easy, but securing enterprise data access without slowing development is hard. Teams often rely on static, over-privileged credentials, creating major security risks. But there’s a solution. This session explores how to secure agentic workflows with Amazon Bedrock AgentCore and Okta. We’ll introduce the Blueprint Alliance, of which AWS and Okta are founding members, and cover how standards like Cross App Access (XAA) and SCIM for Agents transform AI identity. You'll learn how replacing static keys with dynamic, delegated permissions enables real-time policy evaluation at every tool call, empowering developers to build scalable, trusted AI agents that operate strictly within verified boundaries. This presentation is brought to you by Okta, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC233-S": {"abstract": "Ephemeral AI agents and cloud workloads spin up, access resources, and vanish in minutes, never landing in traditional identity registries. How do you govern what you can’t track? The answer lies in securing the non-human identities (NHIs) they leave behind. Join SailPoint for a live demo on governing ephemeral agent behavior. We’ll show you how to untangle NHI ownership, expose over-privileged access, and establish real-time governance over transient machine identities before they expose your cloud. Walk away with a blueprint to manage non-human access risk at cloud scale. This is clarity you can act on. This presentation is brought to you by SailPoint, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC234": {"abstract": "The AWS European Sovereign Cloud is built on fully isolated infrastructure within the EU, operated exclusively by EU-resident staff, with no access from outside Europe. In this lightning talk, learn from the AWS European Sovereign Cloud Managed Operations team what independent operations means in practice and how it works day to day.", "language": "en"},"SEC235": {"abstract": "Authorization to your key involves a mix of key policies, IAM policies, grants, SCPs, and VPC endpoint policies — each playing a distinct role in access decisions. This talk demystifies how these policies are evaluated in the context of AWS KMS, then applies that understanding to create a scalable policy design. You'll learn about ABAC, using grants for dynamic delegation, and AWS Resource Access Manager (RAM) which offers a simpler yet scalable way to share keys in your organization. You'll leave with the mental model to predict outcomes and reusable patterns that grow with your organization.", "language": "en"},"SEC236-S": {"abstract": "The breach is underway. Watch CrowdStrike Experts put cloud detection and response into action during a live, audience-driven investigation of a simulated AWS intrusion. They will uncover evidence, determine the next move, and capture flags hidden across identities, workloads, and cloud activity. Get briefed on cloud-focused adversary ALTERED SPIDER, then connect the clues, contain the threat, and stop the attack. This presentation is brought to you by CrowdStrike, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC237": {"abstract": "As organizations put AI and agents into production, leaders need confidence that their cloud provider is secure by design. This session explains how AWS foundational security extends into AI services and which AI security capabilities build on that foundation. You see the controls customers use to protect their most critical AI workloads.", "language": "en"},"SEC285-S": {"abstract": "Engineering teams lose time correlating telemetry, triaging alerts, investigating threats, and turning findings into fixes. This session explores how Datadog’s purpose-built AI agents can reduce that operational burden by applying organizational context and production telemetry to focused developer, SRE, and security workflows. Using the AWS Well-Architected Framework, we’ll examine where agents can improve operational excellence, reliability, security, performance efficiency, and cost optimization. This presentation is brought to you by Datadog, an AWS Partner.", "language": "en"},"SEC301-R": {"abstract": "For most security teams, the delay sits in manual triage and tool switching after an alert. This chalk talk covers the newest AWS detection and response capabilities, including enhanced threat detection, automated vulnerability management, and AI-assisted security operations, and how they shorten the time between finding a risk and resolving it. We work through how to connect AWS security services across AWS and your other clouds so the team you already have can resolve findings faster.", "language": "en"},"SEC301-R1": {"abstract": "For most security teams, the delay sits in manual triage and tool switching after an alert. This chalk talk covers the newest AWS detection and response capabilities, including enhanced threat detection, automated vulnerability management, and AI-assisted security operations, and how they shorten the time between finding a risk and resolving it. We work through how to connect AWS security services across AWS and your other clouds so the team you already have can resolve findings faster.", "language": "en"},"SEC302": {"abstract": "Agentic workloads are reaching production, and governance has to keep up without slowing teams down. This session shows how to build a governance foundation for AI with AWS Control Tower, AWS Config, AWS Organizations, and automatic evidence collection, then extend it to autonomous agents. You apply preventive and detective controls to Amazon Bedrock and Amazon SageMaker, automate compliance evidence, and map controls to frameworks such as ISO 42001. The same controls hold whether the workload is a single model call or a fleet of agents acting on their own.", "language": "en"},"SEC303": {"abstract": "AI agents plan and execute actions autonomously, at machine speed, with real-world consequences. Unlike traditional automation that follows pre-defined network paths, agent traffic is non-deterministic, calling different APIs and chaining different services each time it runs. In this session, we cover the network security capabilities AWS has built for this shift. You'll see how to inspect and control autonomous traffic at every layer, from DNS resolution to application-layer filtering, enforce least-privilege connectivity for agents, and detect anomalous behavior in machine-to-machine communication.", "language": "en"},"SEC304": {"abstract": "Organizations struggle to detect vulnerabilities before code reaches production. This session demonstrates how to use AWS Continuum (formerly known as AWS Security Agent) directly into your CI/CD pipeline. Learn how to shift security left, catch vulnerabilities at commit and build stages, surface actionable remediation guidance that prevents vulnerable code from reaching production, and accelerate secure software delivery without slowing developer velocity.", "language": "en"},"SEC305": {"abstract": "In this one-hour builder session, get hands-on with the core capabilities of Amazon GuardDuty Malware Protection. Configure on-demand S3 scanning using the SendObjectMalwareScan API, set up automated EBS volume scanning for EC2 instances, and build event-driven response pipelines that quarantine infected objects before they spread. Walk through real malware samples triggering findings, interpret scan results, and integrate with Security Hub for centralized visibility. Leave with a working malware detection and response workflow deployed in your own account.", "language": "en"},"SEC306-R": {"abstract": "AWS Organizations policies drive governance through native service integrations. In this session, explore service control policies and resource control policies for data perimeters. Use declarative policies to enforce resource configurations, such as Amazon S3 public access restrictions, and centralize AWS Security Hub settings org-wide. Deploy AWS Config organizational rules and conformance packs for continuous compliance, leverage multi-party approval, enforce best practices via the AWS Control Tower controls catalog, and explore automated compliance assessments. Leave with a governance architecture driven by policy integrations.", "language": "en"},"SEC306-R1": {"abstract": "AWS Organizations policies drive governance through native service integrations. In this session, explore service control policies and resource control policies for data perimeters. Use declarative policies to enforce resource configurations, such as Amazon S3 public access restrictions, and centralize AWS Security Hub settings org-wide. Deploy AWS Config organizational rules and conformance packs for continuous compliance, leverage multi-party approval, enforce best practices via the AWS Control Tower controls catalog, and explore automated compliance assessments. Leave with a governance architecture driven by policy integrations.", "language": "en"},"SEC307": {"abstract": "Across complex cloud environments, security teams must sift through a growing volume of signals to prioritize the risks that matter most. AWS Security Hub correlates these signals across AWS and multicloud environments to surface critical attack paths and help you respond at scale. Hear firsthand from SCHUFA, Germany's main credit scoring agency, on how they adopted Security Hub to streamline security operations under strict regulatory requirements. We'll also dive into the latest capabilities, including multicloud visibility, enhanced risk correlation, AI-powered detections, and visibility into AI workloads.", "language": "en"},"SEC308-R": {"abstract": "Determining which KMS keys are actively in use and which can be safely retired has historically required manual CloudTrail log analysis, and meanwhile, unused keys accumulate as operational clutter. In this hands-on builders session, you'll use the GetKeyLastUsage API to identify which keys are protecting production workloads and which are candidates for retirement. You'll configure policy conditions that prevent accidental deletion of active keys, and build a Amazon Quick dashboard that surfaces usage and cost optimization opportunities across accounts. You leave with a working multi-account key lifecycle solution you can recreate in your organization.", "language": "en"},"SEC308-R1": {"abstract": "Determining which KMS keys are actively in use and which can be safely retired has historically required manual CloudTrail log analysis, and meanwhile, unused keys accumulate as operational clutter. In this hands-on builders session, you'll use the GetKeyLastUsage API to identify which keys are protecting production workloads and which are candidates for retirement. You'll configure policy conditions that prevent accidental deletion of active keys, and build a Amazon Quick dashboard that surfaces usage and cost optimization opportunities across accounts. You leave with a working multi-account key lifecycle solution you can recreate in your organization.", "language": "en"},"SEC309-R": {"abstract": "Level up your firewall policy operations at scale in this hands-on workshop. Learn to integrate AWS Network Firewall into an existing AWS environment using Terraform. Starting with a realistic multi-VPC setup with AWS Transit Gateway, you will deploy Network Firewall and configure routing, inspection, and logging in minutes using reusable modules. You will then implement the latest Network Firewall features such as web category filtering, JA4 fingerprinting, and EventBridge automation. By the end of this session you will have both practical experience adding Network Firewall to existing environments and reusable patterns ready for your own infrastructure.", "language": "en"},"SEC309-R1": {"abstract": "Level up your firewall policy operations at scale in this hands-on workshop. Learn to integrate AWS Network Firewall into an existing AWS environment using Terraform. Starting with a realistic multi-VPC setup with AWS Transit Gateway, you will deploy Network Firewall and configure routing, inspection, and logging in minutes using reusable modules. You will then implement the latest Network Firewall features such as web category filtering, JA4 fingerprinting, and EventBridge automation. By the end of this session you will have both practical experience adding Network Firewall to existing environments and reusable patterns ready for your own infrastructure.", "language": "en"},"SEC310": {"abstract": "Most AI agent demos work on stage but break in production. Real enterprise agents need multi-layer guardrails, tenant identity isolation, graceful degradation, and continuous evaluation. In this code talk, we share patterns from shipping two production security agent systems on AWS: one that investigates incidents across customer accounts, and one that helps security practitioners author threat detections. We cover practices that any team can adopt such as prompt injection defense, tenant-scoped sessions, circuit breakers, observability, and eval-driven improvement.", "language": "en"},"SEC311": {"abstract": "Build a Well-Architected workload with three AI agents working design-to-runtime for always-on AI governance. In this builders' session, combine Security Agent (now part of Continuum) for pre-deployment checks, DevOps Agent for triage and root-cause analysis, and Bedrock AgentCore for orchestration via MCP tools. You'll scan for OWASP Top 10 issues, run pen tests, and use ready-to-merge fix PRs. You'll author a skill encoding your runbooks, catch a privilege escalation bug that manual review missed, witness DevOps Agent diagnose an incident, and ship a complete loop: design, code review, adversarial tests, CI/CD gates, monitoring, AI-driven RCA, mapped to a Well-Architected pillar.", "language": "en"},"SEC312": {"abstract": "AI coding tools can make developers 10–20x more productive and yet many organizations may only be able to pentest critical apps once a year. The rest need to go untested entirely. In this builders' session, use AWS Security Agent (now part of Continuum) to run an on-demand penetration test against a live application. You'll triage findings by exploitability, validate real-world impact, and apply automated code remediations. Leave with prescriptive next steps to scan your own applications using a frontier AI agent that secures workloads from design through deployment. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"SEC313": {"abstract": "AWS Network Firewall now gives you the flexibility to act as an explicit forward proxy for outbound traffic on the firewall you already run. Clients connect to it directly by its proxy FQDN, so no route-table changes are required. The proxy attaches to a NAT gateway and terminates each client connection, letting one firewall consolidate egress for multiple VPCs regardless of overlapping CIDRs. In this builders session you'll get hands-on deploying the proxy and building layered egress controls, up to and including outbound TLS inspection. You'll leave knowing when and how to use the firewall proxy to centralize and enforce egress controls for private workloads.", "language": "en"},"SEC315-R": {"abstract": "In this hands-on workshop, master AWS network security techniques using AWS Network Firewall and Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall. Gain practical experience configuring URL category filtering to detect unauthorized AI service usage, and explore recent feature improvements for container workload protection and advanced DNS-layer defenses. Through real-world scenarios, you'll implement IDS/IPS filtering, east-west traffic controls, and domain-based policies using fully managed AWS services. Leave equipped to build resilient network defenses that address both traditional and emerging threats.", "language": "en"},"SEC315-R1": {"abstract": "In this hands-on workshop, master AWS network security techniques using AWS Network Firewall and Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall. Gain practical experience configuring URL category filtering to detect unauthorized AI service usage, and explore recent feature improvements for container workload protection and advanced DNS-layer defenses. Through real-world scenarios, you'll implement IDS/IPS filtering, east-west traffic controls, and domain-based policies using fully managed AWS services. Leave equipped to build resilient network defenses that address both traditional and emerging threats.", "language": "en"},"SEC316": {"abstract": "The migration to post-quantum cryptography is underway and here's a chance to hone your skills. In this gamified builders' session, each team races a countdown clock through different cryptographic migration challenges: TLS endpoint migration, ML-DSA key for code signing, quantum ready PKI, and an audit of your PQ posture. Teams complete quests using the AWS Key Management Service (KMS), AWS Private Certificate Authority, and AWS Secrets Manager services. A live leaderboard tracks progress across teams. Leave with new skills and the confidence to build quantum-resistant cryptographic infrastructure.", "language": "en"},"SEC318-R": {"abstract": "What if your customers' data needs to stay invisible even to you? Cryptographic computing makes computation on encrypted data without decrypting it possible. Fully homomorphic encryption (FHE) has become practical for ML inference. In this session, learn what FHE is, why it now works at scale on AWS, and how to get started. We will walk through two examples: encrypted ML inference on medical images and financial transactions, and encrypted ETL that aggregates records without exposing individual values. Leave with a deployment blueprint built for regulated industries.", "language": "en"},"SEC318-R1": {"abstract": "What if your customers' data needs to stay invisible even to you? Cryptographic computing makes computation on encrypted data without decrypting it possible. Fully homomorphic encryption (FHE) has become practical for ML inference. In this session, learn what FHE is, why it now works at scale on AWS, and how to get started. We will walk through two examples: encrypted ML inference on medical images and financial transactions, and encrypted ETL that aggregates records without exposing individual values. Leave with a deployment blueprint built for regulated industries.", "language": "en"},"SEC319-R": {"abstract": "Are you looking to prevent unintended access to your data and mitigate data exposure risks? This hands-on session demonstrates how data perimeter controls implemented through service control policies (SCPs), resource control policies (RCPs), and VPC endpoint policies help keep corporate data within organizational boundaries. You'll build data perimeter policies for real-life security scenarios and validate their effectiveness using an assessment tool. By the end, you'll have practical knowledge on how to help ensure only trusted identities access trusted resources from expected networks.", "language": "en"},"SEC319-R1": {"abstract": "Are you looking to prevent unintended access to your data and mitigate data exposure risks? This hands-on session demonstrates how data perimeter controls implemented through service control policies (SCPs), resource control policies (RCPs), and VPC endpoint policies help keep corporate data within organizational boundaries. You'll build data perimeter policies for real-life security scenarios and validate their effectiveness using an assessment tool. By the end, you'll have practical knowledge on how to help ensure only trusted identities access trusted resources from expected networks.", "language": "en"},"SEC321-R": {"abstract": "AI coding agents optimise for task completion, not security, generating code faster than humans can review. This chalk talk presents a defence-in-depth framework spanning five phases - pre-generation, authoring, PR, pipeline, and deployment - with three lines of defence at each: deterministic controls, AI screening, and human judgment. We cover author-time techniques (steering, specs, scoped MCP, hooks) and build-time gates (SAST, SCA, IaC scanning, AI-assisted review, approval) using Kiro, AWS Security Agent, and CodePipeline. Leave with a layered model you can apply on Monday.", "language": "en"},"SEC321-R1": {"abstract": "AI coding agents optimise for task completion, not security, generating code faster than humans can review. This chalk talk presents a defence-in-depth framework spanning five phases - pre-generation, authoring, PR, pipeline, and deployment - with three lines of defence at each: deterministic controls, AI screening, and human judgment. We cover author-time techniques (steering, specs, scoped MCP, hooks) and build-time gates (SAST, SCA, IaC scanning, AI-assisted review, approval) using Kiro, AWS Security Agent, and CodePipeline. Leave with a layered model you can apply on Monday.", "language": "en"},"SEC322": {"abstract": "As organizations adopt generative AI on AWS, security teams need to extend their investigation capabilities into the AI layer. In this hands-on workshop, participants work through a realistic multi-phase incident — tracing a compromised credential from initial exposure through privilege escalation into Amazon Bedrock knowledge bases and AI agents. Using GuardDuty, Security Hub, CloudTrail, and Athena, you'll reconstruct the full attack chain and walk away with the skills to detect, investigate, and respond when threat actors pivot from traditional cloud resources into generative AI workloads.", "language": "en"},"SEC323-R": {"abstract": "Most organizations have backups. Few can prove clean recovery after ransomware, and regulators now demand exactly that proof. In this hands-on workshop, you configure a resilient backup architecture on AWS using vaults and immutable storage. You then experience a simulated ransomware event, validate that your recovery infrastructure held, restore to an isolated clean room environment, and scan recovered resources for attacker persistence using GuardDuty Malware Protection. You leave with a recovery validation approach and an understanding of why least privilege, vault lock, and validation are non-negotiable.", "language": "en"},"SEC323-R1": {"abstract": "Most organizations have backups. Few can prove clean recovery after ransomware, and regulators now demand exactly that proof. In this hands-on workshop, you configure a resilient backup architecture on AWS using vaults and immutable storage. You then experience a simulated ransomware event, validate that your recovery infrastructure held, restore to an isolated clean room environment, and scan recovered resources for attacker persistence using GuardDuty Malware Protection. You leave with a recovery validation approach and an understanding of why least privilege, vault lock, and validation are non-negotiable.", "language": "en"},"SEC325": {"abstract": "Your edge traffic is a mix of customers, AI agents, scrapers, and attackers. Treat them the same and you lose either security or revenue. In this workshop, you turn AWS WAF into a decision layer that classifies traffic in real time and routes it. Legitimate users get a fast lane, suspicious traffic goes to deception environments that waste attackers' time and reveal their intent, and AI agents get their own treatment that opens the door to new ways of charging for access. None of it requires changes to your origin logic.", "language": "en"},"SEC326": {"abstract": "AI is accelerating your release cadence. AWS application security capabilities help engineering teams ship secure applications faster, including AI-powered workloads. In this session, learn how AI-driven services enforce organizational guardrails before code is written, perform threat modeling, review code for vulnerabilities, generate fixes automatically, and enable on-demand penetration testing. See how to codify these into paved pathways that give developers room to build with AI while maintaining consistent security standards. Leave with a clear plan for embedding AWS application security into your platform, from design through continuous validation.", "language": "en"},"SEC327": {"abstract": "Multi-agent systems create a larger attack surface than single-agent deployments: prompt injections that propagate across agent boundaries, identity spoofing, tool-chain escalation, and data exfiltration through natural-language covert channels. In this chalk talk, we threat-model this risk using the OWASP Agentic AI Top 10, then whiteboard defense-in-depth patterns for validating inter-agent messages, enforcing least-privilege orchestration, and detecting anomalous behavior before it cascades. Leave with a set of recommendations and architectures that demonstrate how to layer security controls in a multi-agent system.", "language": "en"},"SEC328-R": {"abstract": "Your security team defines the controls, and your product teams build and deploy AI applications across dozens or hundreds of AWS accounts. This chalk talk shows how to enforce AI guardrails centrally without making your security team the bottleneck. We walk through an architecture for applying Amazon Bedrock Guardrails through controls at the AWS Organizations, account, and AI infrastructure layers. You see how controls inherit across those layers, which limitations to plan for, and how tiered governance lets teams tailor controls to their applications while the organizational baseline stays enforced.", "language": "en"},"SEC328-R1": {"abstract": "Your security team defines the controls, and your product teams build and deploy AI applications across dozens or hundreds of AWS accounts. This chalk talk shows how to enforce AI guardrails centrally without making your security team the bottleneck. We walk through an architecture for applying Amazon Bedrock Guardrails through controls at the AWS Organizations, account, and AI infrastructure layers. You see how controls inherit across those layers, which limitations to plan for, and how tiered governance lets teams tailor controls to their applications while the organizational baseline stays enforced.", "language": "en"},"SEC329": {"abstract": "The speed AI gives your developers is also available to attackers, so AI investment is now a security decision as much as an engineering one. This session covers three changes to how security teams work when engineers pair with frontier agents. Agents carry application context across design, code, and pen testing, so security coverage keeps pace with development. When an agent builds a working proof of exploit for a finding, or shows that an existing control already blocks it, the security team no longer has to argue over whether the finding is real, and the false-positive queue shrinks. Agentic development environments also let you put preventive controls on what agents generate. We cover how to adopt these practices and which signals show they're working.", "language": "en"},"SEC3300": {"abstract": "Your workloads and agents authenticate to external systems today mostly via individual OAuth implementations, secrets in config files, and custom refresh logic owned by separate teams. In this chalk talk, we'll explore patterns for centralizing how workloads obtain, manage, and govern credentials for non-native services. We'll diagram a reference architecture covering control, data, and governance planes, walk through real-world authentication flows and discuss how to design policies that control which workloads can access which external credentials.", "language": "en"},"SEC3301": {"abstract": "Discover how to automate and manage TLS certificates using AWS Certificate Manager. In this chalk talk, we'll discuss automation opportunities for certificate issuance, export, distribution, and renewal across diverse environments. By attending, you'll gain the skills to eliminate manual certificate management tasks and minimize the risk of certificate-related outages in your organization.", "language": "en"},"SEC3303": {"abstract": "Writing and maintaining firewall rules at scale can be complex. In this code talk, join AWS Network Security experts as they walk through practical techniques, and new capabilities, to simplify rule-writing experience with AWS Network Firewall. Through live examples, learn to structure rule groups to meet common use cases and deploy according to best practices. This session will dive deep to keep policy writing easy to manage as your environment grows. Walk away with hands-on patterns you can apply immediately to tame rule complexity and ship changes with confidence.", "language": "en"},"SEC3305": {"abstract": "When an IAM request is denied, teams can spend valuable time finding the root cause. Now, access denied error messages go beyond surfacing the policy type or ARN that blocked a request—they tell you the exact reason access was denied. In this session, we'll walk through how the feature works, how to put it to use, and considerations for larger AWS environments where different policy types are owned by different teams.", "language": "en"},"SEC3306": {"abstract": "You remediate a finding today and see the same class of finding next week. This chalk talk shows how to break that cycle. You take a real AWS Security Hub finding category and work through four control layers. An SCP stops the misconfiguration, an AWS CloudFormation Guard rule catches it before deployment, an AWS Config rule detects existing violations, and AI-generated auto-remediation corrects drift. Each finding you remediate this way becomes a rule that keeps it from coming back.", "language": "en"},"SEC331": {"abstract": "Vulnerability management is evolving beyond detection. As AI agents generate and deploy code at machine speed, security teams need a fundamentally different approach to identifying, prioritizing, and resolving software vulnerabilities. In this session, explore how AWS thinks about the future of vulnerability management — from contributing to open source vulnerability databases and detecting malicious packages before they reach your pipeline, to enforcing remediation at scale without slowing development velocity. Learn how to shift from reactive CVE chasing to a proactive posture that accounts for AI-generated code, autonomous deployments, and the expanding software supply chain.", "language": "en"},"SEC332": {"abstract": "Every organization has finite security resources. The more the platform handles, the more your team can focus on securing what is unique to your application — your data, your logic, your users. In this session, we explore the security capabilities built into the AWS platform that work before you write a single IAM policy. From threat intelligence that blocks malicious actors at the network edge, to Nitro's zero operator access architecture that eliminates an entire class of insider risk, to automated reasoning that mathematically proves your policies behave as intended — these are protections you inherit just by running on AWS. We also cover PrivateLink for eliminating public internet exposure, default encryption, and S3 Block Public Access. Understand what the platform already handles so you can direct your security investment where it matters most: the application layer.", "language": "en"},"SEC333": {"abstract": "When an alert fires, your first question is \"what else happened?\" Answering it means correlating API calls, network flows, DNS queries, and application logs, often across dozens of accounts and regions. In this session, we show how security practitioners centralize every signal into a single queryable layer using Amazon CloudWatch unified data store, then investigate incidents with faceted queries that span all telemetry types at once. We walk through building always-on visibility dashboards, running cross-account investigations without switching tools, and using AI-assisted analytics to surface context before you need to go looking for it. You leave with a reference architecture for turning centralized logs into faster investigations and clearer security posture.", "language": "en"},"SEC334": {"abstract": "Threat actors are evolving faster than ever, leveraging AI-generated exploits and automated attack tooling to move at machine speed. AWS CIRT sees these techniques firsthand across customer incidents of all sizes. The Threat Technique Catalog for AWS (TTC), extending the MITRE ATT&CK framework, documents these recurring TTPs with detection and mitigation guidance specific to AWS. This session covers how to turn observed attacker patterns into an advantage: build actionable detections and mitigations into your security operations before the next incident hits.", "language": "en"},"SEC335-R": {"abstract": "The tools to raise the bar on incident response are here. AI can handle the volume so your team can focus on the nuance. But making that shift in practice, earning trust, building feedback loops, securing the tools themselves, is where we have seen teams get stuck. In this chalk talk, AWS Customer Incident Response Team (CIRT) leaders share exactly how we made that transition and what we learned along the way. We'll diagram the pipeline live, simulate an investigation, and spend real time working through your scenarios at the whiteboard.", "language": "en"},"SEC335-R1": {"abstract": "The tools to raise the bar on incident response are here. AI can handle the volume so your team can focus on the nuance. But making that shift in practice, earning trust, building feedback loops, securing the tools themselves, is where we have seen teams get stuck. In this chalk talk, AWS Customer Incident Response Team (CIRT) leaders share exactly how we made that transition and what we learned along the way. We'll diagram the pipeline live, simulate an investigation, and spend real time working through your scenarios at the whiteboard.", "language": "en"},"SEC336": {"abstract": "\"Living off the land\" (LOTL) attacks use native OS tools to evade detection. In 2026, techniques evolved to \"Living off the Agent\" (LOTA), compromising enterprise AI agents through subtle prompt injections to exfiltrate data, escalate privileges, and move laterally. Red team studies identified vulnerabilities in production AI agents with distinct LOTA patterns. This chalk talk explores the LOTA taxonomy, detection strategies, and architectural containment patterns. Bring your architecture diagrams, and we'll whiteboard defenses together.", "language": "en"},"SEC337-R": {"abstract": "Security Hub prioritizes critical security risks and helps you respond at scale. By correlating threats, vulnerabilities, and misconfigurations across AWS and multicloud environments, it surfaces attack paths and prioritizes what needs attention first. In this chalk talk, we deep dive into how exposures work, how risk prioritization scores are calculated, and how to operationalize the findings in your environment.", "language": "en"},"SEC337-R1": {"abstract": "Security Hub prioritizes critical security risks and helps you respond at scale. By correlating threats, vulnerabilities, and misconfigurations across AWS and multicloud environments, it surfaces attack paths and prioritizes what needs attention first. In this chalk talk, we deep dive into how exposures work, how risk prioritization scores are calculated, and how to operationalize the findings in your environment.", "language": "en"},"SEC338-R": {"abstract": "In a live demo, we deploy malware built from APT41 tradecraft onto a Kubernetes cluster and watch an AI agent autonomously investigate the forensic evidence — acquiring volatile memory, identifying injected code, and tracing the attack chain without human intervention.", "language": "en"},"SEC338-R1": {"abstract": "In a live demo, we deploy malware built from APT41 tradecraft onto a Kubernetes cluster and watch an AI agent autonomously investigate the forensic evidence — acquiring volatile memory, identifying injected code, and tracing the attack chain without human intervention.", "language": "en"},"SEC340": {"abstract": "How do you secure that which has no network? AWS services such as S3, SQS, and DynamoDB help teams build and scale faster but come with a different set of security implications than services that live in your VPC. In this session, you'll learn about best practices and architectural patterns for building secure networks on AWS using network features like DNS firewall, and Network firewall and layering them with AWS IAM policies like VPC endpoint policy, and Resource Control Policy to help ensure that your resources that live outside of your network behave like they are in your network.", "language": "en"},"SEC341": {"abstract": "Attendees build a defense-in-depth security architecture for an AI agent. You start with a pre-deployed Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway with Lambda tools, then run adversarial test scenarios to observe how gateway and tools respond without controls. You layer defenses from Bedrock Guardrails for prompt-attack detection, through AgentCore Policy Engine with Cedar policies-based tool authorization, including Dogwood temporal policies, and finally native rate limits paired with CloudWatch anomaly detection. By progressing through each scenario again, you verify how each protection aligns with a layered security pattern you can apply to your agentic workload.", "language": "en"},"SEC342": {"abstract": "In 2026, a frontier AI model demonstrated it could discover and chain software exploits at machine speed. This new reality forced the industry to confront an entirely new class of offensive capability. In this session, learn how AWS Security's Office of the CISO engaged executive customers and partners to build playbooks to help assess AI-era vulnerability management and remediation. We share the lessons implemented with customers, the internal changes AWS made to operate as a more secure AI-first organization, and practical steps every security team can take now.", "language": "en"},"SEC343": {"abstract": "MCP is becoming the standard for connecting AI agents to tools, but MCP servers are packages that present supply chain risk. In this builders session, you will observe a live MCP tool poisoning attack, then build three defense layers hands-on: gateway validation with Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Policy to restrict tool actions, a pre-deployment integrity scanner that detects silent tool definition changes, and runtime monitoring that flags anomalous tool behavior. Leave with a working understanding of how MCP supply chain attacks work and how to defend against them.", "language": "en"},"SEC344": {"abstract": "Agents access tools, call APIs, and delegate to other agents. They need layered identity controls at every stage. In this builders' session, apply zero trust principles to a multi-agent environment using Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. You'll configure agent identities with OAuth providers, enforce least privilege via OBO token exchange, differentiate agents from humans with IAM MCP condition keys, and set Gateway rules blocking unauthorized access. Then, you stress test your setup using cross-chain access, privilege escalation, identity spoofing to see each blocked by a different layer you built.", "language": "en"},"SEC345": {"abstract": "Cryptography is changing fast. The dedicated HSM you rely on today, whether AWS CloudHSM or an on-premises appliance, carries operational overhead that will only grow as the industry moves toward post-quantum cryptography (PQC) and stricter digital sovereignty requirements. AWS Key Management Service (KMS) now covers most workloads that once required dedicated HSMs, with the same FIPS 140-3 Level 3 security, lower cost, and zero infrastructure management, while giving you a clear on-ramp to PQC and the sovereignty controls to keep your keys and data under your control. In this chalk talk, bring your architecture and we'll work through it together: we'll assess your workload, map signature and encryption migration paths, and show how moving to KMS puts you on the fastest path to post-quantum readiness. Meet the AWS Cryptography team and leave with a plan and concrete next steps.", "language": "en"},"SEC346": {"abstract": "In this session, we introduce the OpenClaw architecture, compare the major variants and their security posture differences, and then map the threat landscape for AWS-hosted deployments. Drawing on years of experience helping customers deploy, we cover the infrastructure and runtime threat landscape. You will leave with a reference architecture that applies defense-in-depth principles to such OpenClaw deployments on AWS, minimizing risk without sacrificing capability.", "language": "en"},"SEC347": {"abstract": "When a security event hits a customer account, every minute counts. Learn how AWS CIRT built an agentic AI system using Amazon Kiro that performs investigation workflows autonomously: ingesting case data, baselining environments, analyzing indicators, and producing triage assessments. We cover architecture decisions for AI agents with live AWS API access, evaluation frameworks measuring accuracy against expert answer keys, and guardrails keeping humans in the loop. Leave with patterns for applying agentic AI to operational workflows where accuracy is non-negotiable.", "language": "en"},"SEC348": {"abstract": "When agents and automated pipelines deploy at machine speed, manual sign-off can't keep up, so the proof has to be built into the pipeline itself. Developers want to ship fast, governance teams want controls enforced, and compliance teams want evidence. In this builder session, you'll build a CI/CD pipeline that satisfies all three: cfn-guard validation against policy rules, CloudFormation deployment, Config evaluation after deployment, and a compliance report generated as a pipeline artifact. The full policy-to-proof loop in one pipeline. You leave with a working pipeline where every deployment validates governance rules and produces audit evidence on its own.", "language": "en"},"SEC349": {"abstract": "Your teams are building agents faster than anyone can review them by hand, and that creates operational and security risk along with the visibility problem. In this workshop, you build a layered controls stack for an agent fleet. You enforce content and business-logic boundaries with Amazon Bedrock Guardrails, apply an evaluator pattern for policy validation, and track compliance posture with AWS Config conformance packs. Then you connect these into an operating model that approves routine actions automatically and sends only the ambiguous ones to a person, so you can deploy more agents than your team could review individually.", "language": "en"},"SEC350-R": {"abstract": "Your agents are reviewing PRs, shipping code, and answering messages. They communicate on your behalf, surfacing every weak relationship in your org. Soft skills have never mattered more. Shift Left and Shift Down brought security in as automation to build trust in systems. Agents have moved the question to trust between humans. Security teams have historically operated at a remove: by org chart, tooling, and reputation. That distance becomes a liability when agents reduce the time between decision and consequence. This session is about building a security function whose core output is shared judgment with the engineers it serves.", "language": "en"},"SEC350-R1": {"abstract": "Your agents are reviewing PRs, shipping code, and answering messages. They communicate on your behalf, surfacing every weak relationship in your org. Soft skills have never mattered more. Shift Left and Shift Down brought security in as automation to build trust in systems. Agents have moved the question to trust between humans. Security teams have historically operated at a remove: by org chart, tooling, and reputation. That distance becomes a liability when agents reduce the time between decision and consequence. This session is about building a security function whose core output is shared judgment with the engineers it serves.", "language": "en"},"SEC351": {"abstract": "When AI agents and automated pipelines ship changes across AWS, Azure, and other providers in minutes, governance that runs on separate toolchains can't keep pace. You end up with disparate security findings along with mis-aligned operational metrics, and a team that spends its time reconciling dashboards instead of actually reducing risk. In this session, see how to collapse multicloud governance into a single operating model across AWS and Azure, leveraging Amazon CloudWatch unified telemetry for centralized observability and AWS Security Hub for aggregated findings into a single surface.You'll walk through a deployable architecture that gives one analyst a unified view across clouds, so your governance moves at the same speed your engineering teams already do.", "language": "en"},"SEC352-R": {"abstract": "Teams deploying AI agents inherit two exposures. Any client reaching the endpoint can invoke the agent, and it can reach any destination its tools name. In this builders' session, learn to close both gaps with AWS managed controls. Put AWS WAF Bot Control in front of your Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway to classify callers and admit only the classes you choose, then route the agent through AWS Network Firewall and use domain category filtering to decide which destination categories it may reach. Run each control in count or alert mode, then enforce it and verify the allowed and the refused path. Leave with a two-boundary pattern you can apply to your own agent deployments.", "language": "en"},"SEC352-R1": {"abstract": "AI agents built on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore accept untrusted input and make outbound API calls, creating both ingress and egress attack surfaces. In this session, learn how to defend both paths using AWS WAF and AWS Network Firewall. See how WAF authenticates callers, enforces rate limits, and blocks prompt-injection payloads before they reach your agent, while Network Firewall applies managed threat-intelligence categories to drop egress traffic to malicious destinations without maintaining custom deny lists. Walk away with a deployable defense-in-depth boundary pattern for your agentic AI workloads.", "language": "en"},"SEC354": {"abstract": "Authentication is the front door to your app, and if that's unavailable, everything is unreachable. Amazon Cognito is the managed CIAM service that handles sign-up, sign-in, federation, and threat protection at scale. In this chalk talk, learn how Cognito's new multi-Region replication (MRR) unlocks resilient authentication across Regions. We'll whiteboard reference architectures for B2C, B2B, and M2M workloads, cover implementation considerations like customer managed keys and in-Region dependencies, and walk through failover strategies. Leave ready to architect authentication that keeps customers connected.", "language": "en"},"SEC355": {"abstract": "Workloads across AWS, on premises, and other clouds need verifiable identity without long-lived secrets, and SPIFFE provides it. In this lightning talk, we show how to issue SPIFFE SVIDs and how they flow into AWS. You follow one SVID through SPIRE attestation, cross-cloud federation, exchange for STS credentials through IAM Roles Anywhere or AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity, and presentation to Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Identity for AI agent tool access.", "language": "en"},"SEC356": {"abstract": "Your identity provider handles authentication, but how do you design the authorization model that follows? In this chalk talk, learn how to use OIDC token claims from providers, including Amazon Cognito, with a Cedar-based authorization model. We'll walk through implementing RBAC, ReBAC, and ABAC patterns locally or in Amazon Verified Permissions, and show how to use AI coding agents (Kiro, Claude) to generate your schema and policies. Bring your use case for this interactive session. You'll leave with a repeatable approach to designing fine-grained authorization from scratch.", "language": "en"},"SEC357": {"abstract": "NIST has announced post-quantum cryptography (PQC) standards and CNSA 2.0 requires quantum-resistant algorithms by 2027. IoT devices deployed today operate for 10–20 years, so they need to be quantum-resistant from day one. In this workshop, migrate a real ESP32-S3 SoC from classical TLS to post-quantum cryptography over the air with zero physical access required. Connect to AWS IoT Core, trigger an OTA delivering ML-KEM and ML-DSA with quantum-safe certificates from AWS Private CA, and watch your device reboot into PQC-secured MQTT. No embedded experience needed. You leave with the ESP32-S3 and open-source code to quantum-proof your fleet at scale.", "language": "en"},"SEC358": {"abstract": "Your board wants to know your detections work and auditors want evidence. The only way you find out today is when something real hits. In this code talk, you;ll see us build a detection validation program that answers the question before it gets asked. We'll live-code realistic attack scenarios mapped to MITRE ATT&CK techniques, generate the correlated signals that trigger GuardDuty runtime findings, scope detection rules to specific accounts, and interpret findings as they fire. You'll walk out with a repeatable framework your team can run after every GuardDuty update to prove coverage, not assume it.", "language": "en"},"SEC359": {"abstract": "Static credentials remain the most common source of identity-related security incidents, yet many workloads still rely on them across trust boundaries. AWS now eliminates them in both directions. Humans and workloads outside AWS can use OIDC, SAML, or X.509 certificates to federate in and obtain temporary AWS credentials. Conversely, IAM outbound identity federation lets AWS workloads exchange IAM identities for short-lived JWTs when calling external clouds like Azure and Google Cloud, AI providers like OpenAI or Anthropic, SaaS platforms like Databricks and Snowflake, and your own applications. This session covers how to configure trust, scope credentials, and enforce authorization controls.", "language": "en"},"SEC360": {"abstract": "Learn how AWS approaches identity as the foundation of cloud security - both protecting AWS infrastructure and enabling customers to operate securely at scale. Explore how AWS innovates in access management and develops prescriptive, service-specific guidance helping organizations adopt services confidently, drives secure-by-default authorization across 300+ services, and ensures IAM policies work predictably. Millennium Partners will share how they use AWS guidance and threat modeling to evaluate new services, strengthen their security posture, and give developers freedom to innovate faster.", "language": "en"},"SEC361": {"abstract": "AI agents and automated pipelines now change your infrastructure faster than any review can keep up, and not every change comes through CloudFormation. In this workshop, you'll close the compliance loop end to end: author a CloudFormation Hook that enforces encryption and tagging before deployment, then extend the same logic into an AWS Config custom rule with CloudFormation Guard so out-of-band changes get caught continuously. You'll build auto-remediation with SSM Automation, package it into a Conformance Pack for org-wide reach, and use Resource Timeline to trace drift. You leave able to keep compliance closed however fast change arrives.", "language": "en"},"SEC362": {"abstract": "In this session you will learn how to secure identities and permissions for AI agents across your agentic workflows. Using Strands SDK, Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Identity, Cognito, and Verified Permissions, you implement user and machine authentication, fine-grained authorization, consent management, and permission delegation for agents, tools, and LLMs. The session follows a \"choose your own adventure\" format so you focus on the scenarios most relevant to you, whether that is agent-to-agent delegation, human-in-the-loop consent, or least-privilege tool access. You leave ready to apply these patterns to your own agentic AI systems.", "language": "en"},"SEC363": {"abstract": "DNS is where threats are first visible and Amazon Route 53 DNS Firewall is turning that visibility into defense. This session explores how Route 53 DNS Firewall's evolving security capabilities help you resolve, inspect, and protect egress DNS query traffic to the internet across global, multi-account environments. See how deeper integration with other AWS security services, expanded DNS threat detection, wider visibility, and a growing partner ecosystem come together to strengthen your first line of defense. Whether you run a single VPC or a global network across AWS and hybrid networks, you'll leave with a clear picture of how DNS security for egress traffic is advancing and how to put it to work.", "language": "en"},"SEC364": {"abstract": "Coupang, South Korea's largest e-commerce platform, runs millions of daily transactions on AWS. At that volume, each open source library, container base image, and AI model weight pulled from the internet is a supply chain risk. This session goes deep on the AWS Network Firewall architecture and its newer capabilities, including centralized management and container-aware egress inspection. Coupang shows how it built egress control that scales automatically across a container-first fleet, automates allowlist management, and limits exposure to rogue AI dependencies and dynamic payloads without slowing developers down.", "language": "en"},"SEC365": {"abstract": "Most AWS workloads send traffic out in two ways, DNS queries and network connections, and attackers can abuse both to exfiltrate data. This session shows how to lock down both paths. We cover DNS Firewall enhancements for the DNS path, Network Firewall improvements for outbound connection control, and how running DNS Firewall and Network Firewall together gives you one view of DNS security. We also cover URL and domain categories, managed threat intelligence, and extending egress protection to containerized and AI workloads.", "language": "en"},"SEC366": {"abstract": "AWS gives you flexibility to design your multi-account environment, but customer needs guidance choosing the right security services to deploy, where, and how to validate the deployment against AWS best practices. This session shows how the AWS Security Reference Architecture provides a prescriptive security blueprint you layer on top of your account governance foundation. Using whiteboarding, you cover OU design, service prioritization, centralized vs. distributed placement, design considerations, validation with SRA Verify, and deployment acceleration. You leave with a tailored governance model ready for implementation.", "language": "en"},"SEC367": {"abstract": "Regulatory frameworks like SOC 2, ISO 27001, PCI DSS, and the EU AI Act each demand separate assessments, yet a significant portion of their requirements overlap. You spend time collecting duplicate evidence for controls you already have. This chalk talk introduces the control inheritance approach and shows how a single AWS Config rule or organizational policy can satisfy requirements across multiple frameworks. You walk through real examples of framework overlap, automation patterns that eliminate duplicate evidence collection, and a governance approach you can implement immediately.", "language": "en"},"SEC368": {"abstract": "With automated traffic now more than half of the web, a single firewall layer can't separate good bots from L7 attacks. This chalk talk covers how AWS WAF and AWS Network Firewall work together. We cover deployment patterns for internet-facing apps, outbound controls, and workload segmentation, including firewall patterns attached to AWS Transit Gateway. Then we dig into the tradeoffs, including centralized versus distributed inspection, cost versus latency, and when one service can carry the load and when you need both. Bring your architecture diagrams, and we whiteboard a design for your environment.", "language": "en"},"SEC369": {"abstract": "Most migrations add governance controls after workloads land on AWS, and that creates rework. This chalk talk shows how to build guardrails into your AWS Transform migration workflow with AWS Organizations, IAM Identity Center, AWS Config, and AWS Security Hub so workloads arrive with controls in place. Bring your migration challenges, and we design a secure-by-default landing together that carries governance through each phase of the migration.", "language": "en"},"SEC370": {"abstract": "Enterprises go through mergers, face divestitures, and inherit multiple AWS Organizations, each with its own structure and identity provider. Consolidating or separating these environments is complex. This interactive session covers patterns for merging AWS organizations, managing multiple organizations in parallel, and splitting organizations cleanly. You dive into the identity challenge of migrating between Microsoft Entra ID, Google, and Okta when each entity brings its own IdP to AWS. You leave with practical patterns and lessons learned engagements with customers like you.", "language": "en"},"SEC371-R": {"abstract": "Your network grew faster than your documentation. Routes were added in a sprint, VPC attachments stacked during migrations, and somewhere along the way, your team lost the full picture. When something breaks, you're troubleshooting from memory and stale diagrams. In this session, learn how to turn that around in hours instead of months. See how to point AI-assisted tooling at your live environment to map every resource, trace every traffic path, and surface misconfigurations before they become incidents. Then take it further: modernize what you find with current AWS network security capabilities. Walk out with a repeatable workflow you can run against your own network this week.", "language": "en"},"SEC371-R1": {"abstract": "Your network grew faster than your documentation. Routes were added in a sprint, VPC attachments stacked during migrations, and somewhere along the way, your team lost the full picture. When something breaks, you're troubleshooting from memory and stale diagrams. In this session, learn how to turn that around in hours instead of months. See how to point AI-assisted tooling at your live environment to map every resource, trace every traffic path, and surface misconfigurations before they become incidents. Then take it further: modernize what you find with current AWS network security capabilities. Walk out with a repeatable workflow you can run against your own network this week.", "language": "en"},"SEC372": {"abstract": "When you manage hundreds of AWS accounts, governance can fall behind. Drift accumulates, controls take days to propagate, and problems go undetected until something breaks. We demonstrate Landing Zone Accelerator 2.0 with built-in AI that lets operators use natural language to apply organizational changes in minutes, if not seconds. We deploy org-wide security controls, provision accounts, and enforce encryption through a Bedrock-powered interface supporting CloudFormation and Terraform. An AI agent generates each change plan, and before anything is applied, an org-wide preview catches conflicts and policy violations. Issues are surfaced up front instead of discovered after a break. Attendees learn how we built LZA 2.0 with Kiro spec-driven development and Rust, and how to design pre-deployment previews that keep multi-account operations safe.", "language": "en"},"SEC373": {"abstract": "AWS Identity services are evolving to meet the demands of modern, multi-account environments. Learn how to use IAM Account Access Manager together with IAM Identity Center multi-Region replication and your existing IAM primitives to enable rule-based role assignment at scale across your organization; without recreating your roles or provisioning model. We'll then walk through how a customer migrated to this architecture, building a resilient identity solution across multiple AWS Regions and account structures without compromising security.", "language": "en"}};
  window.APP_DATA.sessions.forEach(function (s) {
    var x = m[s.session_id];
    if (x) for (var k in x) if (x[k]) s[k] = x[k];
  });
})();
